-- Q21 [Airway Resistance & Airflow] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Airway Resistance & Airflow', 'Air moves through the trachea, whose single lumen has a combined cross-sectional area of about 2.5 cm², at a linear velocity of 400 cm/s during a forceful inspiration. By the level of the terminal bronchioles, the millions of parallel branches together present a combined cross-sectional area of roughly 1,000 cm². Assuming the volume flow rate of air is conserved between these two levels, what is the approximate linear velocity of the air at the terminal bronchioles?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "1 cm/s"}, {"label": "B", "text": "1,000 cm/s"}, {"label": "C", "text": "160 cm/s"}, {"label": "D", "text": "160,000 cm/s"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the continuity equation states that when volume flow rate is conserved, the product of cross-sectional area and linear velocity is constant (A₁·v₁ = A₂·v₂). Solving for the velocity at the terminal bronchioles: v₂ = (A₁·v₁)/A₂ = (2.5 cm² × 400 cm/s) / 1,000 cm² = 1,000/1,000 = 1 cm/s. Because the combined cross-sectional area of the branching airway tree balloons roughly 400-fold from trachea to terminal bronchioles, linear velocity must fall by the same factor, slowing the air to a near standstill and giving oxygen time to diffuse.

(Choice A) is correct: v₂ = (2.5 × 400)/1,000 = 1 cm/s, correctly applying A·v = constant so that the ~400-fold increase in combined area produces a ~400-fold drop in velocity.

(Choice B) is incorrect: 1,000 cm/s is the value of the conserved volume flow rate itself (A₁·v₁ = 2.5 × 400 = 1,000 cm³/s), not a velocity. It results from computing the flow rate and stopping there, forgetting the final step of dividing by the terminal-bronchiole area to convert flow rate back into a linear velocity.

(Choice C) is incorrect: 160 cm/s reverses the reasoning by treating the area increase as only a small correction; it wrongly divides the trachea velocity by ~2.5 (the trachea''s own area value) instead of by the full area ratio of ~400, so the air is left far too fast.

(Choice D) is incorrect: 160,000 cm/s comes from multiplying by the area ratio instead of dividing (v₂ = v₁ × A₂/A₁), which reverses the inverse relationship and absurdly speeds the air up as the airway widens.

Skill: This item tests quantitative reasoning by applying the continuity equation to compute how linear air velocity changes as combined airway cross-sectional area changes.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
