-- Q26 [Inhalation Mechanics] easy skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Inhalation Mechanics', 'A patient sustains an injury that leaves the diaphragm completely unable to contract, though all other respiratory muscles remain intact. During a resting, quiet breath, what is the most direct consequence for air movement into the lungs?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Air intake is unchanged because the diaphragm functions only during forceful exhalation"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Air intake is severely reduced because the primary increase in thoracic cavity volume is lost"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Air intake increases because thoracic pressure rises above atmospheric pressure"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Air is expelled from the lungs because the thoracic cavity shrinks when the diaphragm relaxes"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because during quiet inspiration the diaphragm contracts and flattens downward, which is the main way the thoracic cavity enlarges; a larger cavity lowers the internal pressure below atmospheric pressure so air flows in. If the diaphragm cannot contract, this dominant volume increase is lost, so the ability to draw air in during a resting breath is severely impaired.

(Choice B) is correct: The contracting, flattening diaphragm accounts for the majority of the thoracic volume increase during quiet inspiration; without it, the cavity cannot expand normally, the pressure drop that pulls air in is lost, and air intake is severely reduced.

(Choice A) is incorrect: This reverses the diaphragm''s role. The diaphragm is the primary muscle of inspiration (drawing air in), not a muscle of forceful exhalation; quiet exhalation is largely passive as the diaphragm relaxes.

(Choice C) is incorrect: Inspiration requires thoracic pressure to fall below atmospheric pressure so air moves inward. A pressure rise above atmospheric would push air out, not draw it in, so this describes the opposite of inspiration.

(Choice D) is incorrect: This describes what happens during relaxation/exhalation, not the loss of contraction during an attempted breath in. The question asks about the consequence for drawing air in, which fails because expansion cannot occur.

Skill: This item tests the ability to reason from a functional deficit to its physiological consequence in breathing mechanics.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
