-- Q27 [Inhalation Mechanics] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Inhalation Mechanics', 'During a normal, relaxed breathing cycle at rest, air is drawn into the lungs and then passively released. Which part of this cycle at rest requires skeletal muscle to actively contract in order to occur?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Releasing air from the lungs, which depends on contraction of the abdominal muscles"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Both drawing air in and releasing air out require active muscle contraction equally"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Drawing air into the lungs, which depends on contraction of the diaphragm and external intercostals"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Neither part requires muscle contraction, since air moves entirely by diffusion across the alveoli"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because at rest, drawing air into the lungs is the active phase: the diaphragm and external intercostal muscles contract, enlarging the thoracic cavity, which lowers intrapulmonary pressure below atmospheric pressure so air flows in. Quiet release of air, by contrast, is passive—it results from elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall when those muscles relax, requiring no muscular work. (Choice C) is correct: contraction of the diaphragm and external intercostals is precisely what makes quiet inhalation an active process. (Choice A) is incorrect: at rest, releasing air is passive and driven by elastic recoil; abdominal muscles assist only during forced (active) exhalation, not quiet breathing. (Choice B) is incorrect: the two phases are not symmetric—quiet inhalation is active while quiet exhalation is passive, so they do not require equal muscular effort. (Choice D) is incorrect: bulk air movement into and out of the lungs is driven by pressure differences created by muscle-driven volume changes, not by diffusion; diffusion governs gas exchange across the alveolar membrane, not the movement of air through the airways. Skill: This item tests the ability to identify which phase of the breathing cycle depends on active muscular contraction versus passive elastic recoil.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
