-- Q30 [Inhalation Mechanics] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Inhalation Mechanics', 'During a normal quiet inhalation, the diaphragm contracts and flattens while the external intercostals lift the rib cage, expanding the thoracic cavity. Air then flows into the lungs from the outside environment. Which condition must exist at the instant air begins to enter the airways?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Pressure inside the alveoli is higher than atmospheric pressure"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Pressure inside the alveoli is lower than atmospheric pressure"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Pressure inside the alveoli is exactly equal to atmospheric pressure"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The volume of the thoracic cavity is decreasing as air enters"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because expansion of the thoracic cavity increases lung volume, and by Boyle''s law the increased volume lowers the pressure inside the alveoli below atmospheric pressure; gas then flows down this gradient from the higher-pressure outside air into the lower-pressure lungs. (Choice B) is correct: when the diaphragm and external intercostals enlarge the thoracic cavity, alveolar (intrapulmonary) pressure drops below atmospheric, so the pressure gradient points inward and air is drawn into the lungs. (Choice A) is incorrect: alveolar pressure exceeding atmospheric pressure would drive air outward, which is what occurs during exhalation, not inhalation. (Choice C) is incorrect: when the two pressures are exactly equal there is no gradient and no net airflow occurs; this is the transient equilibrium at the end of a breath, not the condition that initiates inflow. (Choice D) is incorrect: during inhalation the thoracic cavity volume is increasing, not decreasing; a decreasing volume would raise alveolar pressure and expel air. The Skill tested is applying a foundational principle (Boyle''s law and pressure gradients) to predict the direction of airflow during pulmonary ventilation.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
