-- Q31 [Inhalation Mechanics] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Inhalation Mechanics', 'A healthy volunteer voluntarily holds her breath as long as she can. After about a minute she involuntarily gasps and resumes breathing, unable to continue the hold despite trying. Which feature of the muscle driving her inspiration best explains why she was able to suspend breathing at all, yet could not sustain the hold indefinitely?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It is smooth muscle that briefly obeyed conscious effort before its intrinsic pacemaker automatically resumed contraction"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It is cardiac-type muscle whose autorhythmicity cannot be interrupted, so the hold ended the instant automaticity overrode her will"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It is skeletal muscle receiving both somatic motor commands and involuntary drive from central chemoreceptors, and the involuntary drive prevails as blood chemistry shifts"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It is skeletal muscle under purely voluntary control, so the hold ended only when the muscle fatigued and could no longer stay relaxed"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the diaphragm is skeletal muscle innervated by the phrenic nerve and therefore receives voluntary somatic motor commands (allowing the breath-hold) as well as involuntary drive from the respiratory centers, which are driven largely by central chemoreceptors sensing rising CO2 and falling pH; as breath-holding drives CO2 up, that involuntary drive grows until it overrides conscious suppression and forces inspiration. (Choice C) is correct: dual innervation of a skeletal muscle explains both the conscious override (voluntary suspension) and its temporary nature (involuntary chemoreceptor-driven command eventually wins). (Choice A) is incorrect: the diaphragm is skeletal, not smooth muscle, and it has no intrinsic pacemaker; its rhythm is imposed by the brainstem respiratory centers, not a myogenic pacemaker. (Choice B) is incorrect: the diaphragm is not cardiac muscle and is not autorhythmic — if it were truly uninterruptible, a voluntary breath-hold would be impossible in the first place, contradicting the scenario. (Choice D) is incorrect: the hold does not end because of muscle fatigue from staying relaxed (relaxation requires no sustained effort); it ends because the involuntary chemoreceptor-driven inspiratory command overrides the voluntary suppression, so ''purely voluntary control'' cannot account for the involuntary gasp. This item tests the skill of reasoning about the physiological consequences of the diaphragm''s dual (somatic and autonomic) neural control.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
