-- Q33 [Inhalation Mechanics] hard skill4 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Inhalation Mechanics', 'During one moment of a quiet respiratory cycle, a subject''s intra-alveolar (intrapulmonary) pressure is measured at 758 mmHg while atmospheric pressure is 760 mmHg. Which phase is occurring, and what accounts for the direction of airflow at this instant?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Inspiration; thoracic volume has increased, lowering alveolar pressure below atmospheric so air flows into the lungs"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Inspiration; alveolar pressure has risen above atmospheric because the diaphragm has relaxed, driving air into the lungs"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Expiration; thoracic volume has decreased, raising alveolar pressure above atmospheric so air flows out of the lungs"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Expiration; alveolar pressure has fallen below atmospheric because elastic recoil expands the thorax, pulling air out"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the intra-alveolar pressure (758 mmHg) is 2 mmHg BELOW atmospheric pressure (760 mmHg), and air always flows down a pressure gradient from higher to lower pressure — so air is moving INTO the lungs, which defines inspiration. This negative alveolar pressure is produced when contraction of the diaphragm and external intercostals expands the thoracic cavity; by Boyle''s law, increasing the volume of the sealed thoracic/lung space decreases the pressure of the gas within it, dropping alveolar pressure below atmospheric and establishing the inward gradient. (Choice A) is correct: it correctly reads the gradient (alveolar < atmospheric → inward flow → inspiration) AND supplies the correct Boyle''s-law mechanism (volume up → pressure down). (Choice B) is incorrect: it names inspiration but reverses the mechanism — during inspiration alveolar pressure falls BELOW atmospheric (as the data show, 758 < 760), and the diaphragm CONTRACTS rather than relaxes; a pressure risen above atmospheric would drive air outward, not inward. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly links decreased thoracic volume to elevated alveolar pressure and outward flow, but that describes expiration, whereas the data show alveolar pressure BELOW atmospheric (758 < 760), meaning air is flowing in, not out. (Choice D) is incorrect: it inconsistently pairs ''expiration'' with an alveolar pressure below atmospheric (which would actually pull air in) and misapplies elastic recoil, which during expiration causes the thorax to SHRINK and compress the alveoli, not expand them. This question tests the skill of reasoning from quantitative pressure data to the underlying ventilatory mechanism using Boyle''s law.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
