-- Q34 [Inhalation Mechanics] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Inhalation Mechanics', 'At the end of a quiet exhalation, a healthy subject''s respiratory muscles are all relaxed, yet the lungs neither continue to empty nor spring back to a larger size — the system simply rests at a fixed volume. Which condition best explains why the relaxed respiratory system comes to rest at this particular volume?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The outward recoil of the chest wall has ceased entirely, leaving only the lungs'' inward recoil, which has fallen to zero at this volume."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The inward elastic recoil of the lungs is exactly balanced by the outward recoil of the chest wall, so the net force on the system is zero."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The alveolar pressure has become equal to atmospheric pressure, which by itself holds the lungs open at this fixed volume."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The airways have closed off, trapping a fixed volume of gas that the relaxed muscles can no longer move."}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because at the resting end of a quiet exhalation, the lungs'' inward elastic recoil pulling them smaller is exactly opposed by the chest wall''s tendency to spring outward larger; because these two opposing tendencies are equal in magnitude, the net force on the system is zero and the relaxed system settles at this equilibrium volume. (Choice B) is correct: it identifies the true mechanical equilibrium — two opposing elastic recoil forces of equal magnitude cancel, which is exactly why a relaxed system holds a fixed resting volume. (Choice A) is incorrect: the chest wall recoil has not ceased; it is precisely the persisting outward chest wall recoil that opposes the lungs'' inward recoil, and the lungs'' inward recoil is not zero at this volume — both are nonzero and balanced. (Choice C) is incorrect: alveolar pressure does equal atmospheric pressure at end-exhalation (which is why airflow stops), but equal alveolar and atmospheric pressures cannot by themselves hold the lung expanded — the lung is held open by the subatmospheric intrapleural pressure generated by the two opposing recoils, not by the alveolar pressure. (Choice D) is incorrect: the airways remain open at the resting end-expiratory volume; the volume is fixed because opposing elastic forces balance, not because gas is trapped behind closed airways. This question requires reasoning about how balanced opposing forces produce a mechanical equilibrium (analysis of a physiological system rather than recall of a labeled value).', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 90)
