-- Q35 [Exhalation & Elastic Recoil] easy skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Exhalation & Elastic Recoil', 'During a normal resting breath, a subject inhales and then allows the breath to leave without any conscious effort — the chest and abdomen simply settle back as air flows out. Which event drives this stage of the resting breathing cycle?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Forceful contraction of the internal intercostals and abdominal wall muscles"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Contraction of the diaphragm, which flattens and enlarges the thoracic cavity"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Relaxation of the diaphragm and external intercostals, allowing the stretched lung and thoracic wall to recoil"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Contraction of the external intercostals, which lifts the rib cage upward and outward"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because quiet exhalation at rest is passive: the inspiratory muscles (diaphragm and external intercostals) simply relax, and the elastic tissue of the lung and thoracic wall — stretched during inhalation — recoils to its resting size, raising intrapulmonary pressure above atmospheric pressure and pushing air out with no muscular contraction required. (Choice C) is correct: with the inspiratory muscles relaxed, elastic recoil of the lung and chest wall shrinks the thoracic cavity and expels air, which is why resting exhalation costs essentially no muscular effort. (Choice A) is incorrect: contraction of the internal intercostals and abdominal muscles produces active, forced exhalation (as in exercise or coughing), not the effortless exhalation of a resting breath. (Choice B) is incorrect: contraction and flattening of the diaphragm enlarges the thoracic cavity to drive inhalation, the opposite phase of the cycle. (Choice D) is incorrect: contraction of the external intercostals lifts the rib cage to expand the chest during inhalation, not exhalation. Skill: this item tests the ability to identify the physiological mechanism underlying a described phase of the respiratory cycle.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
