-- Q37 [Exhalation & Elastic Recoil] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Exhalation & Elastic Recoil', 'During quiet expiration, the diaphragm relaxes and the elastic tissue of the lungs recoils, reducing the volume of the thoracic cavity. Which change in intrapulmonary (alveolar) pressure results from this volume change, and how does it drive airflow?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Intrapulmonary pressure rises above atmospheric pressure, so air flows out of the lungs"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Intrapulmonary pressure falls below atmospheric pressure, so air flows out of the lungs"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Intrapulmonary pressure falls below atmospheric pressure, so air flows into the lungs"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Intrapulmonary pressure rises above atmospheric pressure, so air flows into the lungs"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because Boyle''s law states that, at constant temperature, the pressure of a fixed quantity of gas is inversely proportional to its volume. When the diaphragm relaxes and elastic recoil shrinks the thoracic cavity, the volume of the lungs decreases, so the intrapulmonary (alveolar) pressure rises above atmospheric pressure; gas always moves down its pressure gradient, so air flows from the higher-pressure alveoli out to the lower-pressure atmosphere.

(Choice A) is correct: Decreasing lung volume raises intrapulmonary pressure above atmospheric (Boyle''s law), and air flows down the pressure gradient out of the lungs — this is expiration.

(Choice B) is incorrect: It correctly identifies outward airflow but reverses the pressure change. A pressure that had fallen below atmospheric would establish an inward gradient, not an outward one, so this pairing is internally inconsistent.

(Choice C) is incorrect: This describes inspiration, not expiration. A falling (sub-atmospheric) intrapulmonary pressure is what occurs when thoracic volume increases during inhalation, drawing air in — the opposite of the volume decrease described in the stem.

(Choice D) is incorrect: The pressure direction is right (above atmospheric), but the airflow direction is wrong. A pressure higher than atmospheric pushes air out, not in; air cannot flow into a region of higher pressure.

Skill: This item tests the ability to apply the inverse pressure-volume relationship (Boyle''s law) to predict the direction of a pressure change and the resulting airflow during a specified phase of breathing.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
