  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Lung Volumes & Capacities', 'After a maximal forced exhalation, roughly 1.2 L of air still remains in a healthy person''s alveoli and cannot be expelled. Which functional consequence of this un-exhaled air best explains why it is physiologically advantageous during normal quiet breathing?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It stores a reserve of oxygen that is drawn upon only during forced exhalation, when fresh inspired air can no longer reach the alveoli"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It supplies the elastic recoil energy that drives passive expiration once the inspiratory muscles relax"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It keeps the alveoli partially inflated between breaths, so oxygenation of pulmonary capillary blood continues even during the expiratory phase rather than only during inspiration"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It prevents the alveolar CO2 level from ever falling low enough to silence the central chemoreceptors that sustain the breathing rhythm"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the residual volume keeps the alveoli from collapsing between breaths, maintaining a standing reservoir of air whose partial pressures continue to drive O2 into (and CO2 out of) the pulmonary capillary blood throughout the entire cycle, so gas exchange proceeds continuously rather than pausing during expiration. (Choice C) is correct: because the alveoli stay inflated with residual air even at the end of a maximal exhalation, the alveolar-capillary partial-pressure gradients persist during expiration, allowing blood to be oxygenated continuously instead of only while fresh air is being inhaled. (Choice A) is incorrect: residual volume is not a selectively tapped store used only during forced exhalation; it is continuously present and continuously exchanging gas, and its value describes air that cannot be exhaled at all, not a reserve mobilized on demand. (Choice B) is incorrect: passive expiration is powered by the elastic recoil of stretched lung tissue and the chest wall, a property of the elastin/collagen fibers, not by the residual volume of air itself; this confuses the driver of expiration with the functional benefit of the air left behind. (Choice D) is incorrect: the breathing rhythm is generated by the medullary respiratory center and modulated by chemoreceptor responses to arterial CO2/H+, and residual volume does not set a floor on alveolar CO2 that keeps this drive active; this misattributes rhythm maintenance to a lung volume. The relevant skill is recognizing the functional significance of a structural or volumetric feature and distinguishing it from the mechanism that produces a different phase of the same process.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
