-- Q40 [Exhalation & Elastic Recoil] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Exhalation & Elastic Recoil', 'In a patient whose disease has destroyed the walls of many alveoli, the elastic tissue that normally stretches during inhalation and springs back afterward is largely lost. During quiet breathing, which change in this patient''s ability to move air is most directly produced by the loss of this springing-back?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Quiet inhalation is impaired, because without recoil the chest wall can no longer expand when the diaphragm contracts."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Quiet exhalation is unchanged, because exhalation during rest is powered by contraction of the internal intercostal and abdominal muscles rather than by recoil."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both quiet inhalation and quiet exhalation are impaired equally, because recoil supplies the pressure gradient that drives airflow in both directions."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Quiet exhalation is impaired and air is retained in the lungs, because the recoil that normally drives passive outward airflow is diminished."}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because quiet (resting) exhalation is a passive process powered by the elastic recoil of the stretched lung tissue; when that elastic tissue is destroyed, the lung no longer springs back forcefully, so air is not expelled efficiently and becomes trapped, retained in the lungs after each breath. (Choice D) is correct: Loss of recoil removes the passive driving force for resting exhalation, so exhalation is impaired and air is retained — the defining consequence of diminished elastic recoil. (Choice A) is incorrect: Inhalation is an active process driven by contraction of the diaphragm and external intercostals, which enlarge the thorax; it does not depend on elastic recoil, so resting inhalation is not the step most directly impaired (in fact, an over-compliant lung is easier, not harder, to inflate). (Choice B) is incorrect: It correctly notes that internal intercostals and abdominal muscles can drive exhalation, but those muscles power only forced exhalation; quiet exhalation at rest is passive and recoil-driven, so losing recoil does impair resting exhalation. (Choice C) is incorrect: Recoil does not drive inhalation — inspiration is muscle-driven — so the deficit is not symmetric; only the passive, recoil-dependent exhalation phase is directly compromised. This item assesses the skill of predicting a physiological consequence from an altered structural property (loss of elastic recoil).', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
