-- Q41 [Exhalation & Elastic Recoil] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Exhalation & Elastic Recoil', 'A researcher measures the collapsing (deflating) pressure of an excised lung under two conditions. When the lung is inflated with air, the total inward pressure driving deflation is 8.0 cm H2O. When the same lung is inflated to the identical volume with saline — which abolishes any air-liquid interface — the inward pressure is 3.0 cm H2O. Which condition would MOST closely reproduce, or exceed, the air-filled measurement if surfactant were washed out of the air-filled lung?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A deflating pressure well above 8.0 cm H2O, because removing surfactant raises surface tension and adds to the recoil."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A deflating pressure of exactly 3.0 cm H2O, because surfactant removal eliminates the tissue''s elastic contribution."}, {"label": "C", "text": "A deflating pressure of 5.0 cm H2O, because only the surface-tension component remains after surfactant is removed."}, {"label": "D", "text": "A deflating pressure below 3.0 cm H2O, because surfactant is what generates surface tension in the first place."}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the total inward (deflating) pressure of the air-filled lung is the sum of two components: the tissue''s intrinsic elastic recoil and the surface tension at the air-liquid interface. The saline-filled measurement (3.0 cm H2O) abolishes the air-liquid interface, so it isolates elastic recoil alone; the difference (8.0 - 3.0 = 5.0 cm H2O) is the surface-tension contribution present in the air-filled lung. Surfactant normally LOWERS that surface tension. Washing surfactant out therefore raises surface tension, increasing the surface-tension component above its normal 5.0 cm H2O value while elastic recoil (3.0 cm H2O) is unchanged — so the total deflating pressure rises above the original 8.0 cm H2O. (Choice A) is correct: elastic recoil (3.0) is unchanged and the surface-tension term grows beyond 5.0, pushing the total past 8.0. (Choice B) is incorrect: 3.0 cm H2O is the elastic-recoil-only value, but surfactant removal does not eliminate tissue elasticity — it acts on the surface-tension term, and removing surfactant raises tension rather than removing it. (Choice C) is incorrect: 5.0 cm H2O is the normal surface-tension component computed from the difference, but it wrongly assumes elastic recoil vanishes and that tension stays at its surfactant-lowered value; in fact the 3.0 recoil persists and the tension rises. (Choice D) is incorrect: surfactant does not create surface tension — the air-liquid interface does; surfactant reduces it, so removing surfactant increases, not decreases, the deflating pressure. Skill: this item tests the ability to partition a measured physiological quantity into its mechanistic components and predict how perturbing one component changes the whole.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 120)
  RETURNING id
)
