-- Q42 [Inhalation Mechanics] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Inhalation Mechanics', 'A patient in severe respiratory distress strains to draw in each breath, and a clinician observes that the muscles of the neck are visibly tensing with every inspiration in a way they do not during quiet breathing. Recruitment of which muscle group most directly assists this labored inhalation, and by what action?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The internal intercostals, by pulling the ribs downward and inward to reduce thoracic volume"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The sternocleidomastoid and scalene muscles, by elevating the sternum and upper ribs to further enlarge the thoracic cavity"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The abdominal wall muscles, by compressing the abdominal contents and driving the diaphragm upward"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The transversus thoracis, by drawing the lower ribs together to stiffen the chest wall against collapse"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because during labored or forced inspiration the sternocleidomastoid and scalene muscles act as accessory muscles of inhalation, lifting the sternum and upper ribs beyond what the diaphragm and external intercostals achieve, which further enlarges the thoracic cavity and lowers alveolar pressure to draw in more air. (Choice A) is incorrect: the internal intercostals pull the ribs downward and inward, which decreases thoracic volume and drives forced exhalation, not inhalation. (Choice C) is incorrect: contraction of the abdominal wall raises abdominal pressure and pushes the diaphragm upward, an action that assists forced exhalation, the opposite of drawing air in. (Choice B) is correct: the sternocleidomastoid and scalene muscles elevate the sternum and upper rib cage during effortful inspiration, adding to thoracic expansion and reinforcing the fall in intrapulmonary pressure that pulls air inward. (Choice D) is incorrect: the transversus thoracis depresses the ribs and contributes to expiration, and stiffening the chest wall does not enlarge the cavity to draw air in. This item tests the skill of identifying which muscle groups are recruited for forced inspiration versus forced expiration based on the direction of rib-cage and thoracic-volume change each produces.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
