-- Q44 [Lung Volumes & Capacities] easy skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Lung Volumes & Capacities', 'A physiologist studies a healthy adult breathing normally into a spirometer. After the subject exhales as forcefully and completely as possible, a measurable amount of air still occupies the alveoli and cannot be pushed out no matter how hard the subject tries. Which lung parameter does this un-expellable air represent?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Expiratory reserve volume"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Tidal volume"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Vital capacity"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Residual volume"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the air that remains in the lungs after a maximal forced exhalation—and which cannot be voluntarily expelled—is the residual volume; it keeps the alveoli inflated and prevents them from collapsing between breaths. (Choice D) is correct: residual volume is the fixed pool of air trapped in the alveoli and airways after the most forceful possible exhalation, exactly matching the scenario of air that cannot be pushed out. (Choice A) is incorrect: expiratory reserve volume is the extra air a person CAN still forcibly exhale beyond a normal (tidal) breath out; because it can be expelled, it is used up before the maximal-exhalation endpoint described here, not left behind. (Choice B) is incorrect: tidal volume is the air moved in or out during a single relaxed, normal breath, which has nothing to do with the air remaining after a maximal forced exhalation. (Choice C) is incorrect: vital capacity is the total volume a person can exhale after a maximal inhalation (IRV + TV + ERV); it is the air that CAN be moved, the opposite of the trapped air described. Skill: This item tests the ability to identify the physiological quantity described by a functional scenario rather than by its label.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
