-- Q47 [Lung Volumes & Capacities] hard skill4 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Lung Volumes & Capacities', 'A pulmonary technician records the following values for a patient at rest: tidal volume = 500 mL, inspiratory reserve volume = 3100 mL, expiratory reserve volume = 1200 mL, and total lung capacity = 6000 mL. What is the patient''s functional residual capacity?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "1200 mL"}, {"label": "B", "text": "2900 mL"}, {"label": "C", "text": "2400 mL"}, {"label": "D", "text": "3600 mL"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because functional residual capacity (FRC) is the volume remaining in the lungs after a normal, passive expiration, equal to expiratory reserve volume (ERV) plus residual volume (RV). RV is not measured directly by spirometry, so it must first be derived: total lung capacity (TLC) = tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume + expiratory reserve volume + residual volume, giving RV = 6000 − (500 + 3100 + 1200) = 1200 mL. FRC = ERV + RV = 1200 + 1200 = 2400 mL. (Choice A) is incorrect: 1200 mL is a single component — the ERV alone, which also happens to equal the derived RV alone; reporting one component omits the other pool of air, and FRC requires summing both the ERV and the residual air that remain in the lungs after a quiet expiration. (Choice C) is correct: RV is recovered as 1200 mL from TLC minus the three measured volumes, and adding it to the 1200 mL ERV yields the 2400 mL FRC. (Choice B) is incorrect: 2900 mL = ERV + RV + tidal volume (1200 + 1200 + 500) improperly folds the tidal volume into the capacity; the 500 mL tidal breath is exhaled during the passive expiration itself, so it is not part of the air still retained after that expiration and must not be added to FRC. (Choice D) is incorrect: 3600 mL = tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume is the inspiratory capacity (IC), the maximal volume that can be inhaled from the end of a quiet expiration; this confuses the volume that can be drawn in with FRC, the volume left behind, so it sums the wrong two components entirely. This item tests the skill of reasoning through multi-step quantitative data to derive an unmeasured value and combine components into a defined respiratory capacity.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
