-- Q48 [Lung Volumes & Capacities] hard skill4 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Lung Volumes & Capacities', 'A technician measures a subject''s lung function with a standard spirometer, which records only air moved into and out of the mouthpiece. From maximal inspiration the subject exhales as forcefully and completely as possible, and the device reports a vital capacity of 4.6 L. A separate helium-dilution test on the same subject reports a total lung capacity of 6.0 L. The spirometer alone cannot account for the missing 1.4 L primarily because that volume:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "is the extra air drawn in during a maximal inspiration beyond a normal quiet breath"}, {"label": "B", "text": "is the additional air forced out during a maximal expiration below the resting expiratory level"}, {"label": "C", "text": "is exchanged so rapidly across the alveolar membrane that the spirometer cannot record it in time"}, {"label": "D", "text": "remains in the lungs even after the most forceful expiration, so it never passes through the mouthpiece"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because a spirometer only registers air that actually flows through the mouthpiece, and the 1.4 L difference between total lung capacity (6.0 L) and vital capacity (4.6 L) is the residual volume — the air that stays in the lungs and airways even after the most forceful possible expiration. Because it is never exhaled, ordinary spirometry cannot capture it, which is exactly why an indirect method such as helium dilution is needed to obtain total lung capacity. (Choice D) is correct: the leftover 1.4 L is air that cannot be expelled and therefore never crosses the sensor, making it invisible to direct spirometry. (Choice A) is incorrect: air inhaled beyond a quiet breath is inspiratory reserve volume, which does move through the mouthpiece during the maximal inspiration and is fully included within the measured 4.6 L vital capacity. (Choice B) is incorrect: air forced out below the resting level is expiratory reserve volume, which is likewise exhaled through the device and already counted in the 4.6 L vital capacity, so it is not the missing volume. (Choice C) is incorrect: gas exchange speed across the alveolar wall does not determine what a spirometer records; the instrument measures bulk airflow at the mouth, and the reason the volume is missing is that it is never exhaled, not that it moves too fast to detect. This item tests the skill of reasoning from quantitative lung-volume data to the mechanism that makes residual volume unmeasurable by direct spirometry.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
