-- Q51 [Neural Control of Breathing] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Neural Control of Breathing', 'A patient sustains isolated damage to a paired cluster of neurons in the pons, above the medulla. Afterward, the patient''s basic breathing rhythm continues, but the normally smooth transition from inhaling to exhaling becomes irregular, with prolonged, gasping inspirations. Which brainstem structure was most likely damaged?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The medullary respiratory center, which normally generates the basic involuntary rhythm that drives the diaphragm"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The peripheral chemoreceptors, which normally sense arterial oxygen levels in the carotid and aortic bodies"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The pontine respiratory groups, which normally modulate the medullary rhythm and smooth the switch from inspiration to expiration"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The central chemoreceptors, which normally sense changes in cerebrospinal fluid pH caused by CO2"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the pontine respiratory groups (the pneumotaxic and apneustic centers) sit in the pons above the medulla and fine-tune the medullary rhythm; damaging them leaves the basic rhythm intact but disrupts the smooth inspiration-to-expiration switch, producing prolonged, gasping breaths. (Choice C) is correct: the pontine centers modulate, rather than generate, the rhythm, so their loss distorts the timing and smoothness of breathing while the underlying rhythm persists. (Choice A) is incorrect: the medullary center generates the basic rhythm, so its destruction would abolish the involuntary drive entirely rather than merely making the transition irregular. (Choice B) is incorrect: the peripheral chemoreceptors sense arterial O2 and lie in the carotid and aortic bodies, not the pons, and their loss would blunt the response to low oxygen, not disrupt the inspiration-expiration transition. (Choice D) is incorrect: the central chemoreceptors sense CSF pH shifts from CO2 and adjust ventilatory drive; their loss impairs CO2 responsiveness rather than the smoothness of the breathing switch. This question tests the skill of identifying the distinct roles of brainstem respiratory structures.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
