-- Q52 [Neural Control of Breathing] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Neural Control of Breathing', 'During a passive lung inflation experiment, a researcher rapidly injects a large volume of air into an anesthetized animal''s lungs and records a burst of afferent action potentials traveling toward the brainstem along the vagus nerve. As these signals arrive, the animal''s ongoing inspiratory effort is cut short and the next inspiration is delayed. Which structures generated the afferent signals, and what is the immediate functional consequence of this reflex?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Central chemoreceptors in the medulla detected the added air volume, and their vagal input prolongs inspiration to expel the excess air"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Pulmonary stretch receptors in the airway smooth muscle fired as the lungs distended, and their vagal input strengthens the inspiratory drive to complete a maximal breath"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Peripheral chemoreceptors in the aortic arch detected the added air volume, and their vagal input inhibits inspiration to lower the rising oxygen level"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Pulmonary stretch receptors in the airway smooth muscle fired as the lungs distended, and their vagal input inhibits further inspiration, guarding against overinflation"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the Hering-Breuer inflation reflex begins when pulmonary stretch receptors embedded in the smooth muscle of the airways are excited by lung distension; they send afferent signals along the vagus nerve to the medullary respiratory center, which inhibits further inspiratory activity and thereby prevents damaging overinflation of the lungs. (Choice D) is correct: it correctly identifies mechanosensitive pulmonary stretch receptors as the source, the vagus as the afferent pathway, and inspiratory inhibition (protection against overinflation) as the consequence. (Choice A) is incorrect: central chemoreceptors in the medulla respond to CO2/H+ in the cerebrospinal fluid, not to physical lung volume, and the reflex terminates rather than prolongs inspiration. (Choice B) is incorrect: although it names the correct receptor and pathway, it reverses the functional outcome—the reflex inhibits inspiration to limit inflation, it does not augment inspiratory drive toward a maximal breath. (Choice C) is incorrect: peripheral chemoreceptors respond to arterial PO2, PCO2, and pH, not to mechanical stretch, and adding air to the lungs is a volume stimulus rather than a change these receptors sense. This question tests the skill of identifying the sensory receptor and reflex arc responsible for a described physiological protective response.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
