-- Q53 [Neural Control of Breathing] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Neural Control of Breathing', 'Two patients each sustain a complete cervical spinal cord transection. Patient 1''s lesion is at the C2 level, and Patient 2''s lesion is at the C6 level. In which patient is diaphragmatic breathing abolished, requiring mechanical ventilation, and why?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Patient 1, because a lesion at C2 lies above the C3–C5 origin of the phrenic nerve and cuts off descending drive to the phrenic motor neurons"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Patient 2, because a lesion at C6 lies above the C3–C5 origin of the phrenic nerve and cuts off descending drive to the phrenic motor neurons"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Patient 1, because a lesion at C2 destroys the medullary respiratory center that generates the breathing rhythm"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Patient 2, because a lesion at C6 severs the phrenic motor neurons themselves within the C3–C5 cord segments"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the phrenic nerve arises from spinal cord segments C3–C5 ("C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive"), so a complete transection at C2—above these segments—interrupts the descending drive from the brainstem to the phrenic motor neurons and paralyzes the diaphragm, making the patient ventilator-dependent; a C6 lesion lies below C3–C5 and therefore spares the phrenic outflow and diaphragmatic breathing. (Choice A) is correct: a C2 lesion is rostral to the C3–C5 phrenic motor pool, cutting the pathway carrying the medullary rhythm to those motor neurons, so the diaphragm loses its drive and cannot contract. (Choice B) is incorrect because a C6 lesion is below, not above, the C3–C5 origin; it leaves the phrenic outflow intact, so diaphragmatic breathing is preserved in Patient 2. (Choice C) is incorrect because the medullary respiratory center lies in the brainstem, well above the spinal cord; a cervical cord lesion at C2 leaves the medulla intact and rhythm generation continues—the problem is that this rhythm can no longer reach the phrenic neurons. (Choice D) is incorrect because a C6 lesion is caudal to the C3–C5 segments and does not sever the phrenic motor neurons themselves; those cell bodies and their outflow remain intact and functional. This question tests the skill of reasoning from spinal anatomy to predict how the level of a cord lesion determines whether diaphragmatic ventilation is preserved or lost.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
