-- Q56 [Chemoreceptor Regulation] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Chemoreceptor Regulation', 'A researcher selectively severs the glossopharyngeal and vagal afferent nerves carrying signals from the carotid and aortic bodies to the brainstem, while leaving the medulla and its central chemoreceptors fully intact. Which ventilatory response is this animal now LEAST able to mount?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "An increase in ventilation in response to a rise in the CO2 dissolved in the cerebrospinal fluid bathing the medulla"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A decrease in ventilation when arterial PCO2 falls well below normal after voluntary hyperventilation"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An increase in ventilation driven by acidification of the interstitial fluid surrounding the medullary neurons"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A rapid increase in ventilation in response to a sharp drop in arterial PO2 to 55 mmHg"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the ventilatory response to low arterial PO2 (hypoxia) is detected almost exclusively by the peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies, which relay their signals to the medulla through the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves; cutting these afferents abolishes the animal''s ability to sense arterial hypoxia, so it can no longer raise ventilation when PO2 falls. The central chemoreceptors, which remain intact, respond to CO2 and pH changes in the fluid around the medulla but are insensitive to O2, so those responses are preserved. (Choice D) is correct: peripheral chemoreceptors are the sole functional sensors of arterial hypoxia, and their afferent pathway runs in cranial nerves IX and X, so severing those nerves eliminates the hypoxic ventilatory drive. (Choice A) is incorrect: the response to CO2 rising in the cerebrospinal fluid around the medulla is mediated by the intact central chemoreceptors, which do not depend on the severed peripheral afferents. (Choice B) is incorrect: a fall in PCO2 below normal reduces central chemoreceptor drive directly at the medulla, decreasing ventilation independently of the peripheral nerves that were cut. (Choice C) is incorrect: acidification of the interstitial fluid around medullary neurons stimulates the central chemoreceptors, whose signaling is intrinsic to the brainstem and unaffected by loss of the peripheral afferent pathway. This question tests the skill of reasoning about how disrupting a specific sensory pathway isolates the unique function of the peripheral chemoreceptors from that of the central chemoreceptors.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
