-- Q57 [Neural Control of Breathing] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Neural Control of Breathing', 'A researcher records rhythmic action potentials from the medullary respiratory center of an anesthetized mammal. After administering a drug, the frequency of rhythmic bursts driving the diaphragm and external intercostals drops from 15 to 9 cycles per minute, while tidal volume per breath is unchanged. Assuming dead space stays constant, what is the most likely effect on the animal''s arterial CO2?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Arterial CO2 rises, because reduced ventilation lowers the rate at which CO2 is exhaled relative to its production"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Arterial CO2 falls, because slower breathing gives each alveolus more time to unload CO2 into expired air"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Arterial CO2 is unchanged, because tidal volume per breath was held constant"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Arterial CO2 rises, because the slower rhythm shifts the diaphragm toward the venous side of the pulmonary circuit"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the medullary respiratory center sets breathing rate through its rhythmic output; dropping the burst frequency from 15 to 9 per minute with unchanged tidal volume lowers minute (alveolar) ventilation by about 40%. Because CO2 is continuously produced by metabolism and cleared only by exhaling alveolar gas, cutting the rate of exhalation below the rate of production causes CO2 to accumulate, so arterial (and alveolar) CO2 rises.

(Choice A) is correct: alveolar ventilation = (tidal volume − dead space) × rate. With tidal volume and dead space fixed, dropping the rate from 15 to 9 per minute (a ~40% reduction) reduces alveolar ventilation, so CO2 is removed more slowly than it is generated and its partial pressure climbs — the same mechanism behind hypoventilation-driven hypercapnia.

(Choice B) is incorrect: the notion that slower breaths let each alveolus ''unload more'' inverts the physiology. Alveolar–capillary CO2 equilibration is fast and essentially complete during a normal breath; total CO2 clearance is governed by how much fresh air is moved per minute, and fewer breaths move less air, raising CO2 rather than lowering it.

(Choice C) is incorrect: ventilation depends on both rate and tidal volume. Holding tidal volume constant does not hold minute ventilation constant when rate falls; the reduced number of breaths per minute still decreases total alveolar ventilation and therefore changes CO2.

(Choice D) is incorrect: this invents a routing effect. The rhythmic rate of the diaphragm does not reassign it to the ''venous'' side of the pulmonary circuit; gas exchange occurs at the alveolar–capillary membrane regardless of firing frequency, and the pulmonary vessels'' oxygenation status is not set by respiratory rate.

Skill: This item requires reasoning from a described change in physiological drive to a quantitative gas-exchange consequence rather than recalling a definition.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
