-- Q60 [Chemoreceptor Regulation] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Chemoreceptor Regulation', 'A climber ascends rapidly to high altitude, where low inspired oxygen drives immediate hyperventilation that lowers arterial and CSF PCO2. Over the first day this hypocapnia partially suppresses her ventilatory drive, but after several days at altitude her ventilation climbs higher still. Which change in the cerebrospinal fluid best explains why the central chemoreceptor drive is restored over these days?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Choroid plexus transport raises CSF bicarbonate, further alkalinizing the CSF and directly stimulating the central chemoreceptors"}, {"label": "B", "text": "CSF PCO2 rises back above its sea-level value, re-acidifying the CSF and reactivating the central chemoreceptors"}, {"label": "C", "text": "CSF oxygen tension falls further, allowing the central chemoreceptors to respond directly to the low PO2"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Choroid plexus transport lowers CSF bicarbonate, returning CSF pH toward normal and relieving the initial suppression of the central chemoreceptors"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because central chemoreceptors respond to the H+ (pH) of the CSF, which is set by CSF PCO2 and CSF bicarbonate. Acutely, hypoxia-driven hyperventilation blows off CO2, lowering CSF PCO2 and making the CSF alkaline; this rise in CSF pH suppresses the central receptors and blunts drive. Over days, the choroid plexus actively transports bicarbonate out of the CSF, lowering CSF [HCO3-] so that CSF pH returns toward normal despite the persistently low PCO2. With the alkaline brake removed, the central chemoreceptors resume firing and ventilation climbs—the basis of ventilatory acclimatization. (Choice D) is correct: lowering CSF bicarbonate offsets the low CSF PCO2, normalizing CSF pH and restoring central chemoreceptor drive. (Choice A) is incorrect: raising bicarbonate would make the CSF more alkaline, which suppresses rather than stimulates the central chemoreceptors, and these receptors are stimulated by acidity (H+), not alkalinity. (Choice B) is incorrect: at altitude ventilation stays elevated, so CSF PCO2 remains below its sea-level value; it does not rise back above baseline, and re-acidification comes from lowered bicarbonate, not from rising CO2. (Choice C) is incorrect: central chemoreceptors do not sense oxygen; the peripheral chemoreceptors handle the hypoxic drive, and CSF PO2 changes do not directly activate the central receptors. This item tests the skill of reasoning about how a regulatory setpoint is reset through a slow compensatory adjustment (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving).', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
