-- Q61 [pH Control & the Bicarbonate Buffer] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'pH Control & the Bicarbonate Buffer', 'A patient develops a metabolic acidosis that abruptly raises arterial H⁺ concentration, yet the central chemoreceptors in the medulla respond only weakly and slowly, whereas an equivalent rise in arterial CO₂ stimulates them within seconds. Which property of the blood-brain barrier best accounts for this difference in how quickly the two disturbances reach the central chemoreceptors?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The barrier is freely permeable to dissolved CO₂ but relatively impermeable to H⁺ and HCO₃⁻, so only CO₂ rapidly enters the cerebrospinal fluid and shifts its pH."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The barrier is freely permeable to H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ but blocks CO₂, so metabolic acids reach the receptors faster than a change in arterial CO₂."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The barrier is equally permeable to CO₂, H⁺, and HCO₃⁻, so the slower response to metabolic acid reflects weaker buffering in arterial plasma rather than transport across the barrier."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The barrier actively pumps H⁺ into the cerebrospinal fluid faster than it allows CO₂ to diffuse, amplifying the response to metabolic acids."}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the blood-brain barrier readily permits dissolved CO₂ to diffuse across into the cerebrospinal fluid, where it forms carbonic acid and lowers CSF pH almost immediately, directly stimulating the central chemoreceptors; in contrast, the barrier is relatively impermeable to charged H⁺ and HCO₃⁻, so a metabolic acid load in the arterial blood cannot rapidly alter CSF pH and reaches the central receptors only slowly. (Choice A) is correct: it correctly pairs high CO₂ permeability with low H⁺/HCO₃⁻ permeability, which is exactly why respiratory (CO₂) changes act fast centrally while metabolic acids act slowly. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the true permeabilities—CO₂ crosses readily and H⁺/HCO₃⁻ do not, so this would predict the opposite (fast metabolic, slow CO₂) response. (Choice C) is incorrect: the difference is a transport phenomenon at the barrier, not a matter of plasma buffering; if the barrier were equally permeable to all three, metabolic acid would reach the receptors as fast as CO₂. (Choice D) is incorrect: the barrier does not actively pump H⁺ into the CSF to amplify metabolic signals; H⁺ movement is restricted, which is why the metabolic response is slow, not amplified. Skill: This question tests the ability to reason from a structural property (selective barrier permeability) to a functional physiological consequence (differential speed of central chemoreceptor stimulation).', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
