-- Q66 [Chemoreceptor Regulation] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Chemoreceptor Regulation', 'A researcher perfuses an animal''s cerebrospinal fluid with a solution while keeping arterial blood gases constant. Lowering the pH of the perfusing fluid triggers a strong increase in ventilation, but experimentally dropping arterial oxygen tension to 55 mmHg (with all other variables held constant) produces almost no change in this same ventilatory response. The receptors being stimulated by the acidified fluid are best classified as which type, and why?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Peripheral chemoreceptors, because they are the receptors that monitor the acidity of cerebrospinal fluid rather than of arterial blood"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Central chemoreceptors, because they respond to shifts in H+ within the fluid bathing the brain but are insensitive to arterial oxygen levels"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Peripheral chemoreceptors, because only these receptors can detect a fall in blood pH and they are unaffected by low oxygen"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Central chemoreceptors, because they directly sense the drop in arterial oxygen and relay it to the medulla"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because the receptors respond to H+/pH changes in the fluid surrounding the brain (the CSF) yet do not respond to a large drop in arterial oxygen tension — this profile matches central chemoreceptors, which sense CO2/H+ in the CSF and are the primary driver of ventilation but cannot detect O2. (Choice B) is correct: central chemoreceptors are located in the medulla and respond to H+ generated when CO2 diffuses into the CSF; they have no oxygen-sensing capability, exactly matching the described behavior (strong response to CSF acidification, negligible response to hypoxemia). (Choice A) is incorrect: peripheral chemoreceptors monitor arterial blood, not cerebrospinal fluid, so the location of the stimulus rules them out. (Choice C) is incorrect: peripheral (not central) receptors are the ones that DO respond to low oxygen, so ''unaffected by low oxygen'' misdescribes them; a fall in arterial O2 to 55 mmHg would normally excite peripheral chemoreceptors. (Choice D) is incorrect: central chemoreceptors cannot sense arterial oxygen at all — only peripheral chemoreceptors respond to O2 — so this reverses the actual physiology. Skill: This item requires classifying receptors by matching an experimentally observed stimulus-response profile to the known sensory properties of central versus peripheral chemoreceptors.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
