-- Q67 [Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation] easy skill4 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation', 'In the lungs, the partial pressure of oxygen in the blood entering the pulmonary capillaries is about 40 mmHg, while the partial pressure of oxygen in the alveolar air is about 104 mmHg. What is the primary factor that drives oxygen to move from the alveolar air into the capillary blood?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Active transport of oxygen across the alveolar membrane by carrier proteins"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The greater total air pressure inside the alveoli compared to the blood"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The difference in oxygen partial pressure between the alveolar air and the capillary blood"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The binding of oxygen to hemoglobin pulling gas across the membrane"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because gas exchange at the alveolar-capillary membrane occurs by simple diffusion, and each gas moves independently down its own partial-pressure gradient. Oxygen is at 104 mmHg in the alveolus and only 40 mmHg in the incoming capillary blood, so this 64 mmHg difference in oxygen partial pressure is the driving force that pushes O2 from the alveolar air into the blood. (Choice C) is correct: diffusion is driven by the partial-pressure gradient of the individual gas; oxygen moves from where its partial pressure is high (alveolus) to where it is low (blood). (Choice A) is incorrect: alveolar gas exchange is passive diffusion, not active transport; no carrier proteins or ATP are required to move O2 across the thin respiratory membrane. (Choice B) is incorrect: it is not the total air pressure that matters but the partial pressure of the specific gas; each gas diffuses down its own individual gradient regardless of the total pressure. (Choice D) is incorrect: hemoglobin binding helps maintain the gradient by removing dissolved O2, but the movement across the membrane itself is driven by the partial-pressure difference, not by a pulling force from hemoglobin. Skill: This item requires applying the principle that gases diffuse across the respiratory membrane down their individual partial-pressure gradients.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
