-- Q70 [Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation', 'In one lung region, a mucus plug narrows the airway feeding a cluster of alveoli so that fresh air barely reaches them, while blood flow through the capillaries wrapping those same alveoli continues at its normal rate. Compared with a neighboring region where airflow and blood flow are well matched, the blood leaving the capillaries of the plugged region is most likely to have:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "an unchanged PO2, because capillary blood always equilibrates with alveolar air regardless of how well the alveolus is ventilated"}, {"label": "B", "text": "a lower PO2, because the poorly replenished alveolar air equilibrates to an oxygen level below normal before the passing blood can fully load oxygen"}, {"label": "C", "text": "a higher PO2, because the reduced airflow slows blood transit and gives oxygen extra time to diffuse into the blood"}, {"label": "D", "text": "a lower PO2 caused by a thicker diffusion barrier, since poor ventilation deposits connective tissue across the respiratory membrane"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because oxygen loading into pulmonary capillary blood is driven by the alveolar-to-capillary PO2 gradient, and that gradient depends on the alveolar PO2 being continuously restored by ventilation; when ventilation is reduced but perfusion continues, the well-perfused blood strips oxygen from the trapped alveolar air faster than sluggish airflow can replace it, so alveolar PO2 (and therefore the equilibrated end-capillary PO2) settles below normal, and this ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch is a classic cause of hypoxemia. (Choice B) is correct: with ventilation choked off but blood flow intact, the alveolus cannot maintain a normal PO2, the driving gradient is diminished, and blood leaves with a subnormal PO2. (Choice A) is incorrect: equilibration is against whatever the local alveolar PO2 actually is; when poor ventilation lowers that alveolar PO2, the equilibrated blood is likewise low, so ventilation absolutely matters. (Choice C) is incorrect: it invents extra loading time, but the airway obstruction reduces ventilation to the alveolus, not blood flow through the capillary, and even a longer transit time cannot raise blood PO2 above the depleted alveolar PO2 that sets the ceiling. (Choice D) is incorrect: it misattributes the low PO2 to a thickened membrane, but the barrier here is structurally normal; the defect is a low alveolar PO2 from reduced ventilation, not impaired diffusion across the membrane. The skill tested is applying the ventilation-perfusion matching principle to reason about how a low local V/Q ratio depresses end-capillary PO2 and produces hypoxemia.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', 'Equilibration guarantees a normal value regardless of ventilation state' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'Reduced airflow reframed as reduced blood flow that boosts loading time and raises PO2' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'Correct direction attributed to a diffusion-barrier mechanism instead of V/Q mismatch' FROM q;

-- Q71 [Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation] medium skill4 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation', 'A researcher measures the amount of oxygen physically dissolved in a fixed volume of plasma exposed to alveolar air. She then adjusts the ventilator so that the oxygen partial pressure at the alveolar surface rises from 100 mmHg to 300 mmHg while temperature and plasma volume are held constant. Compared with the original condition, the quantity of oxygen dissolved in the plasma will be approximately:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "unchanged, because plasma quickly becomes saturated at any partial pressure"}, {"label": "B", "text": "1/3 as much"}, {"label": "C", "text": "3 times as much"}, {"label": "D", "text": "9 times as much"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the amount of a gas that physically dissolves in a liquid is directly proportional to that gas''s partial pressure at the liquid''s surface, so tripling the alveolar oxygen partial pressure from 100 mmHg to 300 mmHg triples the quantity of oxygen dissolved in the plasma (300/100 = 3). (Choice C) is correct: a partial pressure increase from 100 to 300 mmHg is a 3-fold increase, and because dissolved gas content scales linearly (directly) with partial pressure, the dissolved oxygen rises 3-fold. (Choice A) is incorrect: physically dissolved gas does not ''saturate'' at a fixed value the way hemoglobin binding sites do; the dissolved amount keeps rising in direct proportion to partial pressure with no plateau, so the quantity does change. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the relationship, treating dissolved amount as inversely proportional to partial pressure; higher surface partial pressure drives more gas in, not less. (Choice D) is incorrect: 9-fold assumes the amount dissolved scales with the square of the partial pressure ratio (3²), but the relationship is linear, not quadratic, so the correct factor is 3. Skill: This item requires applying the proportional relationship between partial pressure and dissolved gas content to predict a quantitative change.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', 'Confusing physically dissolved gas with saturable hemoglobin binding (assumes a plateau where none exists)' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'Treating dissolved amount as inversely proportional to partial pressure instead of directly proportional' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', 'Applying a quadratic (squared) scaling to the pressure ratio instead of linear proportionality' FROM q;

-- Q72 [Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation', 'In a diseased lung, the total alveolar surface area available for gas exchange falls to one-half of its normal value and the average thickness of the respiratory membrane doubles. To partially compensate, a clinician administers a gas mixture that raises the O2 partial-pressure difference across the membrane to three times its normal value. Compared with the normal lung, the rate of O2 diffusion into the pulmonary capillary blood in this diseased lung is now approximately:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "1.5 times as fast (a modest increase)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "3 times as fast (matching the imposed gradient)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "6 times as fast (a large increase)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "0.75 times as fast (a modest decrease)"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the rate of gas diffusion across the respiratory membrane is directly proportional to both the surface area and the partial-pressure gradient, and inversely proportional to the membrane thickness. Multiplying the fold-changes gives a net factor of (surface area x1/2) x (gradient x3) / (thickness x2) = (0.5 x 3) / 2 = 0.75, so diffusion is about 0.75 times the normal rate — a modest net decrease, because the tripled gradient is not enough to overcome the combined penalty of halved area and doubled thickness.

(Choice D) is correct: combining all three effects as (0.5 x 3) / 2 yields 0.75, correctly treating area and gradient as direct factors and thickness as an inverse factor.

(Choice A) is incorrect: 1.5 results from including area and gradient (0.5 x 3 = 1.5) but ignoring the doubled thickness in the denominator; membrane thickness slows diffusion and cannot be omitted.

(Choice B) is incorrect: 3 fixates only on the imposed partial-pressure gradient and ignores the offsetting reductions from halved surface area and doubled thickness; diffusion rate depends on all three factors simultaneously, not the gradient alone.

(Choice C) is incorrect: 6 multiplies gradient (x3) by thickness (x2) as if thickness were a direct factor and drops the halved surface area entirely, reversing the true inverse relationship between thickness and diffusion rate.

This item tests the skill of reasoning from quantitative data to a physiological mechanism by combining multiple simultaneous changes in the determinants of membrane diffusion.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 120)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', 'Omits the inverse thickness factor from the denominator, keeping only the direct factors (0.5x3)' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', 'Fixates on the single imposed gradient change and ignores the other two determinants' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'Treats membrane thickness as a direct (multiplying) factor instead of inverse, and drops the surface-area term' FROM q;

-- Q73 [Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation', 'In a healthy lung, oxygen reaches equilibrium with alveolar gas after a red cell has traveled only about one-third of the way along the pulmonary capillary, so additional uptake for the rest of the transit is negligible. Carbon monoxide, by contrast, is bound so avidly by hemoglobin that its capillary partial pressure stays near zero for the entire transit. Which statement correctly classifies the transfer of each gas and gives the reason?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "O2 transfer is perfusion-limited because equilibration is reached early, so uptake depends on how much blood flows past; CO transfer is diffusion-limited because its partial pressure never rises to shrink the gradient, so uptake depends only on the membrane''s diffusing capacity."}, {"label": "B", "text": "O2 transfer is diffusion-limited because it must cross the membrane, whereas CO transfer is perfusion-limited because hemoglobin removes it so quickly that blood flow becomes the only constraint."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both gases are perfusion-limited because each ultimately binds hemoglobin, so increasing cardiac output would raise the uptake of both to the same degree."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both gases are diffusion-limited because both must traverse the same respiratory membrane, so the thickness of that membrane sets the uptake of each equally."}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because a gas is perfusion-limited when its capillary partial pressure rises to meet the alveolar value partway along the capillary: once equilibrium is reached, no further net transfer occurs, so the amount taken up over a given time is set by how much blood is delivered (perfusion). Oxygen behaves this way in a healthy lung, equilibrating after roughly a third of the transit. A gas is diffusion-limited when its capillary partial pressure never rises enough to shrink the alveolar-to-capillary gradient, so the gradient—and thus net transfer—persists for the whole transit and uptake is capped only by the membrane''s diffusing capacity. Carbon monoxide behaves this way because hemoglobin binds it so avidly that essentially all CO entering the plasma is pulled onto hemoglobin, keeping the dissolved (free) capillary partial pressure near zero throughout.
(Choice A) is correct: it correctly pairs O2 with perfusion-limitation (early equilibration means flow determines uptake) and CO with diffusion-limitation (a persistently near-zero capillary partial pressure keeps the gradient maximal, so only diffusing capacity limits uptake).
(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses both classifications. O2 is not diffusion-limited in a healthy lung precisely because it equilibrates early, and CO is not perfusion-limited because raising blood flow does not relieve its limiting factor—its uptake is set by diffusion, not delivery.
(Choice C) is incorrect: binding hemoglobin does not by itself make a gas perfusion-limited. What matters is whether the capillary partial pressure rises to meet the alveolar value; for CO it does not, so its uptake is limited by diffusion, and increasing cardiac output would not raise CO uptake the way it raises O2 uptake.
(Choice D) is incorrect: sharing the same membrane does not make both gases equally diffusion-limited. In a healthy lung O2 equilibrates well before the end of the capillary, making its uptake flow-dependent rather than membrane-thickness-dependent, so membrane thickness does not set O2 and CO uptake equally.
This question tests the skill of reasoning from a described physiological scenario to classify gas transfer as diffusion- versus perfusion-limited and identify the correct underlying mechanism.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 120)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'Swaps the two classifications, labeling O2 diffusion-limited and CO perfusion-limited' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', 'Assumes hemoglobin binding per se makes a gas perfusion-limited, so both gases respond equally to raised cardiac output' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', 'Assumes a shared respiratory membrane forces identical diffusion-limitation on both gases, ignoring O2''s early equilibration' FROM q;

-- Q74 [Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation] hard skill4 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation', 'Two patients each have an end-pulmonary-capillary (blood leaving the alveoli) PO2 of 70 mmHg, well below normal. In Patient 1, the PO2 of gas inside the alveoli is measured at 105 mmHg; in Patient 2, the PO2 of gas inside the alveoli is 74 mmHg. Both patients have normal alveolar surface area and equal blood flow through the lungs. Which patient''s low blood oxygen is best explained by a thickened respiratory membrane, and why?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Patient 2, because the low alveolar PO2 shows that oxygen is being consumed within the barrier before it can reach the blood"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Patient 1, because a large PO2 difference persists between alveolar gas and the blood leaving the alveoli, indicating oxygen cannot fully cross the barrier despite an ample driving gradient"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Patient 1, because the high alveolar PO2 proves that oxygen is diffusing too rapidly for the blood to retain it"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Patient 2, because the blood PO2 nearly matches the alveolar PO2, confirming that oxygen had to travel an abnormally long distance to equilibrate"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because oxygen crosses the respiratory membrane by simple diffusion, moving from the higher partial pressure in alveolar gas toward the lower partial pressure in capillary blood; the rate of that diffusion falls as the membrane thickens (a longer diffusion distance). In Patient 1 the alveolar PO2 is normal (105 mmHg) yet the blood leaving the alveoli is still only 70 mmHg — a wide ~35 mmHg gap that means a full driving gradient was available but the blood failed to equilibrate with it. Blood that cannot pick up oxygen despite a normal alveolar partial pressure points to a barrier problem (a thickened respiratory membrane), not a gradient problem. In Patient 2 the alveolar PO2 is itself low (74 mmHg) and the blood (70 mmHg) has very nearly equilibrated with it — the membrane transferred oxygen efficiently, but there was little oxygen in the alveolus to transfer, so Patient 2''s defect is a reduced alveolar-to-blood driving gradient, not a thickened membrane.
(Choice B) is correct: a persistent alveolar-to-blood PO2 difference in the face of a normal alveolar PO2 is the signature of diffusion limitation across a thickened membrane — the gradient is present but oxygen cannot cross it fully.
(Choice A) is incorrect: the respiratory membrane does not consume oxygen; it is a passive diffusion barrier. Patient 2''s low alveolar PO2 reflects too little oxygen delivered to the alveolus (poor ventilation or low inspired PO2), not metabolic consumption inside the barrier.
(Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the physiology. A high alveolar PO2 with low blood PO2 signals that too little oxygen crossed, not too much; faster diffusion would make blood PO2 rise toward the alveolar value, not fall.
(Choice D) is incorrect: near-equilibration of blood with a low alveolar PO2 (Patient 2) indicates the membrane worked well over a normal distance; it is the reduced alveolar gradient, not a long path, that limits this patient. A long diffusion distance would leave a wide alveolar-to-blood gap, which is Patient 1''s pattern.
This item tests the skill of reasoning from partial-pressure data to the underlying gas-exchange mechanism.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 120)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', 'Treats the respiratory membrane as metabolically active (consuming O2) rather than a passive diffusion barrier, misreading Patient 2''s low alveolar PO2 as consumption within the barrier.' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'Inverts the diffusion logic — reads a high alveolar PO2 with low blood PO2 as oxygen diffusing too fast to be retained, when it actually means too little O2 crossed.' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', 'Correctly links long diffusion distance to thickened membranes but misapplies it to Patient 2''s near-equilibration, which is the opposite signature (a long path would leave a wide gap, as in Patient 1).' FROM q;

-- Q75 [Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation] medium skill4 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Respiratory System', 'Gas Exchange & Pulmonary Circulation', 'A physiologist samples blood from a vessel just as it enters the capillary network surrounding the alveoli, before any gas exchange has occurred. The sample shows a partial pressure of O2 of 40 mmHg and a partial pressure of CO2 of 45 mmHg, while the air within the adjacent alveoli is measured at a PO2 of 100 mmHg and a PCO2 of 40 mmHg. Which statement best explains why this blood is well positioned to undergo efficient gas exchange?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Both O2 and CO2 diffuse from the alveolar air into the blood, because alveolar air is richer in dissolved gases than the incoming blood."}, {"label": "B", "text": "O2 diffuses from the blood into alveolar air and CO2 diffuses from alveolar air into the blood, restoring the blood to its original composition."}, {"label": "C", "text": "O2 diffuses from alveolar air into the blood and CO2 diffuses from the blood into alveolar air, because each gas moves down its own partial-pressure gradient."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Little net exchange occurs, because the partial pressures of O2 and CO2 in the blood and the alveoli are too similar to drive diffusion."}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the blood entering the alveolar capillaries has a low PO2 (40 mmHg) and a high PCO2 (45 mmHg), while alveolar air has a high PO2 (100 mmHg) and a lower PCO2 (40 mmHg); each gas diffuses independently down its own partial-pressure gradient, so O2 moves from the alveolus into the blood and CO2 moves from the blood into the alveolus. (Choice C) is correct: O2 flows down its gradient (100 to 40 mmHg) into the blood and CO2 flows down its gradient (45 to 40 mmHg) out of the blood, which is exactly the directionality set up by blood that arrives oxygen-poor and CO2-rich. (Choice A) is incorrect: gases do not both move in the same direction; CO2 is actually higher in the blood (45 mmHg) than in the alveolus (40 mmHg), so CO2 leaves the blood rather than entering it, and each gas responds only to its own gradient, not to a total ''dissolved gas'' content. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses both gradients; O2 is lower in the blood than in the alveolus so O2 enters (not exits) the blood, and CO2 is higher in the blood so CO2 exits (not enters) — this exchange oxygenates the blood rather than ''restoring'' its incoming composition. (Choice D) is incorrect: the gradients are not negligible — a 60 mmHg difference for O2 and a 5 mmHg difference for CO2 are sufficient to drive net diffusion, which is precisely why oxygen-poor, CO2-rich blood arriving at the alveoli favors exchange. This item tests the skill of reasoning about diffusion direction from partial-pressure data across the respiratory membrane.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
